    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d934">

                <pb facs="1759" n="1719" sameAs="frus:frus1952-54v09p1/pg1719"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">684A.86/12–1754: Telegram</note>
                <head>No. 934<lb/>The Secretary of State to the Department of State
                </head>
                <dateline>
                    <placeName>Paris</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                        when="1954-12-17T11:00:00+01:00">December 17, 1954—11 a.m.</date>
                </dateline>
                <p rendition="#flush">Top Secret</p>
                <p>
                    Dulte 4. Eyes only Hoover. Secretary and Eden discussed Arab–Israel problem at dinner last night. At
                    Secretary’s request Shuckburgh gave
                    brief résumé his impressions resulting from his recent trip. (With Eden’s acquiescence, he gave us informally
                    thirteen-page report,<anchor n="1" xml:id="d934-f1"/> as yet uncleared by
                    Foreign Office, summarizing his <pb facs="1760" n="1720" xml:id="pg1720"
                    />personal impressions on Arab–Israel dispute and suggestions re a possible
                    settlement. This is being airpouched to Ken
                        Scott, S/S for appropriate
                    distribution.)</p>
                <p>After some further discussion, it was agreed that a U.S.–U.K. WG would be
                    constituted about mid-January to discuss and endeavor to agree on plan for
                    seeking Arab-Israel settlement. It was recognized that absolute secrecy is
                    imperative and that British Embassy would discuss with Department where WG
                    should meet and cover plan to avoid any publicity.</p>
                <p>
                    Jebb raised question of whether French
                    should be informed of this exercise, but it was agreed that this would be
                    unwise. Similarly, there was query whether Turks should be brought in, but it
                    was agreed that in first instance talks must be confined to U.S.–U.K. and that
                    absolute secrecy was imperative. It was recognized that at some subsequent stage
                    others might be brought in to assist in a settlement, but this would depend on
                    results of U.S.–U.K. conversation.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        Dulles
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
